WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.469
IPC systems often handle concurrency even when the feature seems simple.

2
00:00:04.469 --> 00:00:11.782
A server may accept multiple clients, process several requests at once, receive callbacks, or coordinate with worker threads.

3
00:00:11.782 --> 00:00:19.095
Shared state needs synchronization, and each request needs a clear lifecycle: accepted, authorized, validated, executing, cancelled, completed, or

4
00:00:19.095 --> 00:00:19.502
failed.

5
00:00:19.502 --> 00:00:22.346
Ordering should be part of the contract.

6
00:00:22.346 --> 00:00:29.659
Some messages may be independent, while others must follow setup, authentication, capability negotiation, or a previous state transition.

7
00:00:29.659 --> 00:00:34.534
Duplicate, replayed, delayed, or out-of-order messages should have defined behavior.

8
00:00:34.534 --> 00:00:41.847
If idempotency matters, the protocol should say which operations can be retried and which cannot be repeated safely.

9
00:00:41.847 --> 00:00:43.473
Blocking behavior deserves review.

10
00:00:43.473 --> 00:00:52.411
Avoid holding critical locks while waiting for another process, performing I/O, invoking callbacks, or sending messages that may require a response.

11
00:00:52.411 --> 00:00:57.286
Those patterns can create deadlocks, re-entrancy surprises, or service-wide stalls.

12
00:00:57.286 --> 00:01:03.787
Timeouts, cancellation, backpressure, and queue limits keep the system from waiting forever or accepting unbounded work.

13
00:01:03.787 --> 00:01:05.412
Peer failure is normal.

14
00:01:05.412 --> 00:01:13.538
A client may disconnect mid-request, a helper may crash, or a service may shut down while work is pending.

15
00:01:13.538 --> 00:01:20.445
The server should distinguish incomplete, invalid, unauthorized, unsupported, and resource-exceeded messages where useful, then preserve invariants

16
00:01:20.445 --> 00:01:21.663
and release resources.

17
00:01:21.663 --> 00:01:26.539
Privileged operations should not be left half-complete because the peer vanished.

18
00:01:26.539 --> 00:01:29.789
A predictable IPC system is easier to operate.

19
00:01:29.789 --> 00:01:38.321
It has explicit ordering rules, bounded queues, safe cancellation, careful locking, and cleanup paths that are tested under failure rather than

20
00:01:38.321 --> 00:01:39.540
discovered during incidents.

21
00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:48.072
Concurrency tests should include two clients racing for the same resource, cancellation while a request is queued, disconnect during a long

22
00:01:48.072 --> 00:01:50.916
operation, and shutdown while callbacks are pending.

23
00:01:50.916 --> 00:01:53.760
These tests make timing assumptions visible early.
